{{right|''J. F. Cooper'', Oak Openings, p. 28.}}}}
{{smaller block|''Yankee'', in the American use, does not mean a citizen of the United States as opposed to a foreigner, but a citizen of the Northern New England States (Massachusetts, Connecticut, &c.), opposed to a Virginian, a Kentuckian, &c.
{{right|''De Quincey'', Style, Note 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|We have the present ''Yankee'', full of expedients, half-master of all trades, inventive in all but the beautiful, full of shifts, not yet capable of comfort.
{{right|''Lowell'', Biglow Papers, 1st ser., Int.}}}}
'''2.''' By extension, a native of the United States. [Chiefly a European use.] —'''3.''' A soldier of the Federal armies: so called by the Confederates during the war of secession. See ''Yank''<sup>2</sup>.—'''4.''' A glass of whisky sweetened with molasses. ''Bartlett.'' [New Eng.] [Colloq. in all uses.]

'''II.''' ''a.'' Of, pertaining to, or characteristic of the Yankees: as, ''Yankee'' smartness or invention; ''Yankee'' notions.
{{smaller block|Codfish, tinware, apple-brandy, Weathersfield onions, wooden bowls, and other articles of ''Yankee'' barter.
{{right|''Irving'', Knickerbocker, p. 276.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Examine him outside and in, I’d thank ye,
Morals, Parisian — manners, perfect ''Yankee.''</poem>
{{right|''Lord Houghton'', A Knock at the Door (quoted in N. and Q., 7th ser., XI. 106).}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Ez ef we could maysure stupenjious events
By the low ''Yankee'' stan’ard o’ dollars and cents.</poem>
{{right|''Lowell'', Biglow Papers, 2d ser., iv.}}}}
'''Yankee nation''', the United States. [Humorous.]—'''Yankee notions.''' See ''notion.''

'''Yankeedom''' (yang′kē-dum), ''n.'' [< ''Yankee''<sup>2</sup> + ''-dom.''] '''1.''' The region inhabited by Yankees, in any sense of that word.
{{smaller block|Located as it is on the confines of Egypt and of ''Yankee-''}}

