{{smaller block|''dom'' in this State [Illinois], it has done a good work in both sections. {{right|''The Independent'', quoted in Bartlett’s [Americanisms, p. 768.}}}}

'''2.''' Yankees collectively considered.
{{smaller block|Up the turning ''viâ'' Galileo they climb, to the Basilica at the top, . . . hackneyed as only ''Yankeedom'' and Cockneydom, rushing hand in hand through all earth’s sacrednesses, can hackney. {{right|''Rhoda Broughton'', Alas, viii.}}}}

'''Yankee-Doodle†''' (yang′kē-dö′dl), ''n.'' A Yankee: a humorous use, from a popular air so named. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>I might have withheld these political noodles
From knocking their heads against hot ''Yankee Doodles.''</poem>
{{right|''Moore'', Parody of a Celebrated Letter.}}}}

'''Yankeefied''' (yang′kē-fīd), ''a.'' [< ''Yankee''<sup>2</sup> + ''-fy'' + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] Having the appearance or manner of a Yankee; characteristic of a Yankee. [Colloq.]
{{smaller block|The Colonel whittled away at a bit of stick in the most ''Yankeefied'' way possible. {{right|''A Stray Yankee in Texas'', p. 113. (''Bartlett.'')}}}}

'''Yankee-gang''' (yang′kē-gang), ''n.'' An arrangement in a sawmill (in Canada) adapted for logs of 21 inches or less in diameter. {{smaller|It consists of two sets of gang-saws, having parallel ways in the immediate vicinity of each other. One is the slabbing-gang, which reduces the log to a balk and slab-boards. The balk is then shifted to the stock-gang, which rips it into lumber. ''E. H. Knight.''}}

'''Yankeeism''' (yang′kē-izm), ''n.'' [< ''Yankee''<sup>2</sup> + ''-ism.''] '''1.''' Yankee ways or characteristics.
{{smaller block|“I confess I had feared that Lily’s impetuous ways — her — her —” “ ‘Flamboyant ''Yankeeism,'' ’ Mr. Gore-Thompson called it,” suggested Mrs. Clay. “We are from the Southwest originally,” rather stiffly answered Mrs. Floyd-Curtis, who took ''Yankeeism'' to cover the reproach of a New England birthplace. {{right|''Mrs. Burton Harrison'', The Anglomaniacs, i.}}}}

