'''2.''' A locution or a practice characteristic of Yankees, specifically of the inhabitants of New England.
{{smaller block|Cussedness . . . and cuss, . . . in such phrases as “He done it out o’ pure cussedness,” and “He is a nateral cuss,” have been commonly thought ''Yankeeisms''. . . . But neither is our own. {{right|''Lowell'', Biglow Papers, 2d ser., Int.}}}}

'''yanker''' (yang′kėr), ''n.'' [< ''yank''<sup>1</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>. In def. 3 cf. D. ''janker'', a bawler, brawler, lit. yelper, < ''janken'', yelp, bark.] '''1.''' A smart blow.—'''2.''' A great falsehood; a plumper. [Scotch.]
{{smaller block|“Ay, billy, that is a ''yanker!''” said Tam aside. “When ane is gaun to tell a lie, there’s naething like telling a plumper at aince.” {{right|''Hogg'', Three Perils of Man, I. 336. (''Jamieson''.)}}}}
'''3.''' Same as ''yankie'', 2. ''Imp. Dict.''

'''yankie''' (yang′ki), ''n.'' [< ''yank''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ie''<sup>1</sup>, ''-y''<sup>1</sup>. Cf. ''yankee''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' A sharp, forward, clever woman. [Scotch.]—'''2.''' One who speaks or scolds incessantly. ''Imp. Dict.''

'''yanking''' (yang′king), ''p. a.'' [Ppr. of ''yank''<sup>1</sup>, ''v.''] '''1.''' Active; pushing; thoroughgoing. [Scotch.]
{{smaller block|“Ye’ll be nae bagman, then, after a’?” “No,” said the traveller. . . . “Weel, I canna say but I am glad o’ that—I canna bide their ''yanking'' way of knapping English at every word.” {{right|''Scott'', St. Ronan’s Well, ii.}}}}
'''2.''' Jerking; pulling. [U. S.]
{{smaller block|That poor Emery Ann had had a ''yanking'' old horse, and a wretchedly uncomfortable saddle; . . . the wonder was that she had stayed on at all. {{right|''Mrs. Whitney'', Sights and Insights, xxix.}}}}

